1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:02,775
A GPU machine has two memories.

2
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:08,850
The computer's own, which GPU programmers call the host, and the GPU's, called the device.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:10,787
A narrow bridge joins them.

4
00:00:10,787 --> 00:00:18,025
Anything the GPU works on has to cross it first, and the result has to cross back before ordinary code can use it.

5
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:23,412
Here is one real image, 2048 pixels on a side.

6
00:00:23,412 --> 00:00:26,088
Copying it up takes under three milliseconds.

7
00:00:26,088 --> 00:00:29,662
xRep then aligns it on the GPU in about twenty-two.

8
00:00:29,662 --> 00:00:32,037
Copying the result back takes four.

9
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:35,462
Against the CPU's nine seconds the copies are nothing.

10
00:00:35,462 --> 00:00:38,225
Against the GPU's own work, they're not.

11
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:41,825
So it matters how often the data crosses.

12
00:00:41,825 --> 00:00:49,600
If every stage sends its result home and the next one sends it back up, a four-stage chain pays the round trip four times.

13
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,475
If the data stays on the device from the first stage to the last, it pays once.

14
00:00:55,350 --> 00:01:01,338
Every cuPhoton call decides where its result lands, and the choice differs from call to call.

15
00:01:01,338 --> 00:01:05,212
Your agent writes the code, and the code runs either way.

16
00:01:05,212 --> 00:01:11,000
Only knowing where each result lives tells you whether the data crossed once, or at every step.

17
00:01:11,875 --> 00:01:14,475
So ask your agent where each result lives.
